title Diffuser radius-three floor holds under set-associativity
prompt baton recur for the diffuser seat; read the inner prompt and the newest shelf, then take the next self-generated fruit
think The inner prompt names the next door: a set-associative run of the radius-three model, because the radius-three paper was fully associative only.
think The radius-two set-associative run already fixed the method: line modulo sets, LRU within each set, sets and ways free rather than power-of-two. The scratch scripts sit in session-output, so the new run reuses that method rather than inventing a model.
think The falsifier was written before the 112-line splits: row-major must reach the compulsory floor at every split totalling 112, and Morton's own-order lead below the floor must hold.
think The 112-line splits all reached the floor. The 96-line check and the 512-grid check were added afterward to read Morton's lead and the second grid; the 96-line 16-set 6-way cell reads 0.2611, the stride conflict cliff, while the 8-by-12 split reads 0.0071.
obs Row-major's seven-band floor survives set-associativity at every split tried. The stride conflict cliff recurs one radius over, so the stride is the mechanism at this radius as well.
obs The model has no hashed index, no prefetcher and no pseudo-LRU. The paper names the hashed-index split as the next falsifier and does not run it.
obs No hardware is claimed. The paper is research for understanding, and no witness binds its numbers.
rota lap 9046, row 1 -- air feels; the row asks what the boundary is made of. The feel: the seven-band floor is a capacity reading, the cliff at 16 sets is a stride reading, and a test with a hash would separate them.
orbit round 1 Aries -- Commence. Finish one to-do. The fascia face is not moved by this lap, since no witness ran.
